Encapsulation:
Encapsulation is protecting an object's state by hiding its data and allowing controlled access through methods.

A common misconception is that encapsulation means every private field must have a getter and a setter.
Not necessarily.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Abstraction:
Abstraction is the process of exposing only the essential behavior of an object while hiding its implementation details.
 It allows the user to focus on what an object does instead of how it does it.


An abstract class is a partially implemented class that provides common state (variables) and common behavior (implemented methods)
 while allowing child classes to provide the remaining implementation.

*** The abstract keyword in Java is used to declare an incomplete class or an incomplete method that must be completed by its subclass.
Example::
Suppose you're building software for animals.
What is common among all animals?
They eat.
They sleep.
But what is different?
Dog → Barks
Cat → Meows
Lion → Roars
Why use an abstract class?
Because all animals already share code.
There is no need to rewrite:  eat();  sleep();  in every class.




An interface is a contract that specifies what a class must do without specifying how it should do it.
In simple words:
Use an interface when you want different classes to follow the same contract, even if they are completely unrelated.

Example::
Now imagine:
Bird, Airplane, Drone

Are they related?
❌ No.

A bird is not an airplane.
A drone is not a bird.

But they all can: Fly
So we define a contract:
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}


An abstract class is an incomplete class that provides some implementation and leaves the remaining implementation to its child classes.

An interface is a contract that defines a set of methods that implementing classes must provide. It specifies what a class must do, but not how it should do it.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Inheritance:

Inheritance is an OOP mechanism in which a child class acquires the accessible fields and methods of a parent class. 
It promotes code reuse and represents an IS-A relationship between classes.

Inheritance allows one class to reuse the fields and methods of another class instead of writing them again.


super is a reference variable in Java that refers to the immediate parent class object. It is used to access 
the parent class's variables, methods, and constructors.

1. Access Parent varibale     super.name;
2. Access parent Method       super.eat();
3. Access Parent Constructor  super();

Method Overriding :::
Method overriding is a feature of inheritance where a child class provides its own implementation for a method that is already defined in
the parent class with the same method signature.

rules for the method overriding
✅ Same method name
✅ Same parameter list
✅ Same or covariant return type
✅ Cannot reduce access modifier
✅ final methods cannot be overridden
✅ private methods cannot be overridden
✅ static methods are hidden, not overridden
✅ Constructors cannot be overridden
✅ Always use @Override


Types of Inheritance:

1.Single Inheritance
Single inheritance is a type of inheritance in which one child class inherits from one parent class.

2.Multilevel Inheritance:
One class inherits from another, and another class inherits from that.
Multilevel inheritance is a type of inheritance where a class inherits from another derived class.

3.Hierarchical Inheritance:
Multiple child classes inherit from the same parent.
Hierarchical inheritance is a type of inheritance where multiple child classes inherit from a single parent class.


4.Multiple Inheritance:
Multiple inheritance is a type of inheritance where one child class inherits properties and methods from more than one parent class.
Java does not support it with classes to avoid ambiguity caused by the Diamond Problem.

The Diamond Problem occurs when a child class inherits from two parent classes that both inherit from the same grandparent and provide
methods with the same signature. Java cannot determine which method implementation should be used, leading to ambiguity.

          Animal
          sound()

          /      \

Dog               Cat
sound()        sound()

      \         /

        \      /

          Pet
        p.sound()

        if we call sound method in Pet. which method should execute, java cannot decide this ambiguity is called Diamonds problem.


5.Hybrid Inheritance: 
Hybrid inheritance is a combination of two or more types of inheritance.
Java does not support Hybrid Inheritance through classes if it involves Multiple Inheritance. It can be achieved using interfaces.


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

PolyMorphism:
Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take many forms. It allows the same method call to perform different actions depending on the object.

Compile-time polymorphism is a type of polymorphism in which the method to be executed is decided by the compiler during compilation.
It is achieved through method overloading.
The compiler already knows which method to call before the program starts running.
It is also called as Static Polymorphism or Early Binding


Runtime polymorphism is a type of polymorphism in which the method to be executed is decided during program execution. It is
 achieved through method overriding.

It is also called Dynamic Polymorphism or Dynamic Method Dispatch or Late Binding


Upcasting:
Upcasting is the process of assigning a child class object to a parent class reference. It happens automatically in Java 
because every child object IS-A parent object.
Ex: Animal animal = new Dog();

Dynamic Method Dispatching:
Dynamic Method Dispatch is the mechanism by which Java decides which overridden method to execute at runtime based on the actual object.



Downcasting:
Downcasting is the process of converting a parent class reference back to a child class reference. It must be done explicitly.
Animal animal = new Dog();
Dog dog = (Dog)animal;
dog.run();

Method Overriding:
Method overriding is the process in which a child class provides its own implementation of a method that already exists in the parent class
using the same method signature. It enables runtime polymorphism.

Method overloading:
Method overloading is the process of defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists in the same class.
It is used to achieve compile-time polymorphism.

